Local review patch: reject inherited MMU state without local T6032 setup
Base: m1n1 4184923ffb2dff079b384d6a32cc02142aa14572, after 0001..0013
Provenance: AI-assisted Debian project work; not submitted upstream.

An enabled SCTLR.M alone does not establish that T6032 carveout preflight
has run. Track successful local MMU initialization so a first MMU-on entry
cannot silently take the idempotent return path. Clear that state before
a disabled-MMU rebuild, and publish it only after the enable write returns.
Successful repeat calls and temporary disable/restore retain their behavior;
legacy SoCs are unchanged. This is a software lifecycle guard, not adoption
or validation of inherited page tables or protection against privileged
table/register mutation. Panic is not a verified native recovery mechanism.
No firmware execution or new hardware access is authorized by this patch.

--- a/src/memory.c
+++ b/src/memory.c
@@ -47,4 +47,6 @@
 uint64_t ram_base = 0;
+/* An inherited SCTLR.M bit is not evidence of local T6032 carveout setup. */
+static bool t6032_mmu_initialized;
 
 static inline u64 read_sctlr(void)
 {
@@ -590,2 +592,4 @@
     if (read_sctlr() & SCTLR_M) {
+        if (chip_id == T6032 && !t6032_mmu_initialized)
+            panic("T6032: inherited MMU state has not been validated!\n");
         printf("MMU: already initialized.\n");
@@ -596,3 +600,5 @@
-    if (chip_id == T6032)
+    if (chip_id == T6032) {
+        t6032_mmu_initialized = false;
         mcc_t6032_begin_carveout_setup();
+    }
     mmu_init_pagetables();
@@ -614,2 +620,4 @@
     write_sctlr(sctlr);
+    if (chip_id == T6032)
+        t6032_mmu_initialized = true;
     printf("MMU: running with MMU and caches enabled!\n");
